## II. F2 vs. F1: What Feynman's "Uncertainty" Got Compressed Into

**F2 original (Feynman, 1981 BBC interview):**

> "I can live with doubt and uncertainty and not knowing. I think it's much more interesting to live not knowing than to have answers which might be wrong."

**F1 popular version:**

> "I would rather have questions that can't be answered than answers that can't be questioned."

**Key differences:**

| Dimension | F2 | F1 |
|---|---|---|
| **Subject** | "I can live with..." | "I would rather have..." |
| **Emotional tone** | Acceptance, equanimity, curiosity | Confrontation, provocation, binary opposition |
| **Core action** | Living in not-knowing (live not knowing) | Having questions rather than answers (have questions) |
| **Attitude toward "answers"** | "Might be wrong" | "Cannot be questioned" |
| **Context** | Personal epistemological stance | Used as a slogan against dogmatism |

**Why F1 is "better" for the general reader:** It has a clear oppositional structure (questions vs. answers), a clear enemy (answers that cannot be questioned), and a provocative clarity that makes people feel "awake." Its virality on social media far exceeds F2's.

**Why F1 is "lossy":** It transforms the **first-person, emotional, epistemological humility** of F2 into a **third-person, confrontational, moralized stance**. F2 says: "I can live with uncertainty, and that is more interesting than having answers that might be wrong" — a statement about **how to live**. F1 says: "I would rather have questions than answers" — a statement about **what is good**. The former is descriptive; the latter is normative.

**A lossless compressed version of F2 might be:**

> "I can live with uncertainty. Living in questions is more interesting than having answers that might be wrong."

This has less "quote-card punch" than F1, but it preserves the two key qualifiers: "live with" (coexistence) and "might be wrong" (possible error).

